145
00:20:01.590 --> 00:20:24.260
Audio shared by Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): Keys and digital identities come in pairs, a private key and a public key. You keep the private key secret, usually stored in a secure digital wallet. The public key, on the other hand, is meant to be shared, for example, by publishing it in a decentralized identifier, or DID, document, so that others can use it to verify you. With these keys, you can do powerful things.

146
00:20:24.260 --> 00:20:39.359
Audio shared by Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): You can authenticate yourself online and log into services without passwords. You can also digitally sign documents, such as a university signing degrees for its graduates, and anyone can check that signature against the public key. But there is a catch.

147
00:20:39.360 --> 00:20:49.489
Audio shared by Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): If your private key is ever compromised, maybe your wallet gets hacked or the storage system is tricked, your identity could be at risk, meaning that someone could impersonate you online.

148
00:20:49.950 --> 00:20:56.259
Audio shared by Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): That's why it's good practice to rotate your keys frequently, retire the old one, and start using a new one.

149
00:20:56.260 --> 00:21:11.030
Audio shared by Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): In DID systems, these updates may be reported in the DID document data, so that anyone resolving the DID knows which key is current. Still, that raises a problem. What if an attacker steals your key and performs the rotation before you can?

150
00:21:11.030 --> 00:21:18.909
Audio shared by Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): Suddenly, they look like the rightful controller of your did, and you can't reclaim control because the system now trusts their key.

151
00:21:18.910 --> 00:21:32.659
Audio shared by Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): This is where key pre-rotation comes in. With pre-rotation, every time you create a public-private key pair, you also generate the next pair in advance, making sure to keep them secure and available for future use.

152
00:21:32.660 --> 00:21:50.489
Audio shared by Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): Instead of publishing the full future public key, you publish a cryptographic digest of that public key, a commitment to the key pair you'll use next. Later, when you rotate, you reveal the actual public key, and anyone can check that it matches the digest you committed to earlier.

153
00:21:50.490 --> 00:22:05.150
Audio shared by Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): Where this pre-rotation data is stored is specific to each DID method, and is not part of the DIDCORE specification. Some DID methods, like DIDWebS and DIDWebVH, support key pre-rotation, and other methods do not.

